Write $`A'`, $`A` and $`A''` for the Orlik–Solomon algebras of $`\mathcal{A}'`, $`\mathcal{A}` and $`\mathcal{A}''`, and let $`a:A'\to A` be the deletion map.
We know that $`\Phi_{\mathcal{A}}` is surjective, and it remains to show that it is injective, which says that the relations of $`A` are all the relations among the classes $`[f_H]`.

The aim is to compare $`A` with the short exact sequence of {bp "lem:ses"}[], in which $`C(U_{\mathcal{A}})` sits between the cohomology of $`\mathcal{A}'` and of $`\mathcal{A}''`.
So we want $`A''` to describe what $`A` adds to the image of $`A'`, and this is what needs a construction.
A rank count is not an option, because the coefficient ring is arbitrary.

# Why a quotient of modules

The object that should be compared with $`A''` is the cokernel of $`a`.
Since $`a` is a map of unital algebras, $`1` lies in its image, so the two-sided ideal generated by that image is all of $`A`, and the quotient of rings is zero.
The right object is the _module_ quotient
$$`Q=A/\operatorname{im}(a),`
which is not a ring at all.

This explains the shape of {bp "lem:quotient-api"}[the quotient API].
The library's {name OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.DeletionCokernel}`DeletionCokernel` is the quotient of $`A` by the range of $`a` as a linear map, and it carries no algebra structure.
A regression test shows the difference on one hyperplane: over any nonzero ring, the class of the generator is nonzero in this quotient, by {name OrlikSolomon.PresentationExamples.singlePoint_cokernel_generator_ne_zero}`singlePoint_cokernel_generator_ne_zero`, whereas in the quotient by the ideal everything would vanish.

# The linear map from the restriction

The map $`s:A''\to Q` should send a word $`e_{L_1}\cdots e_{L_t}` to the class of $`e_He_{K_{L_1}}\cdots e_{K_{L_t}}`, where $`K_L` is an old hyperplane restricting to $`L`.
This cannot be an algebra map: its target is not an algebra, and it raises degree.
So it is built in three steps.

First, map the exterior algebra on the restricted labels to $`A'` by the chosen old generators, compose with $`a`, multiply on the left by $`e_H`, and project to $`Q`.
This is a linear map out of the exterior algebra, defined with no reference to the relations of $`A''`.

Second, show that it kills the whole relation submodule of $`A''`, including every left and right context $`urv` of every relator $`r`.
Killing the relators alone would be enough for an algebra map, and it is not enough here.
{bp "lem:representatives"}[The relations controlling representatives] are what make this work.
For an empty-intersection relator, the corresponding family with $`H` added also has empty intersection, so the product with $`e_H` vanishes in $`A`.
For a boundary relator, the family with $`H` added is dependent, and its boundary is $`e_I-e_H\partial e_I`, so $`e_H\partial e_I=e_I`, which is an old element and vanishes in $`Q`.
To handle a context, expand the images of $`u` and $`v` in old words and move $`e_H` past them with its exterior sign.
Then each term is zero or old, and so dies in $`Q`.
The linear universal property of {bp "lem:quotient-api"}[the relation quotient] now gives the map on $`A''`.

Third, show that the map does not depend on the representatives.
Two old hyperplanes $`K` and $`K'` with the same trace on $`H` satisfy the triple relation $`e_Ke_{K'}-e_He_{K'}+e_He_K=0`, because the three have a nonempty intersection of codimension two.
So changing one representative changes the value only by an old element, even inside an old word.

:::node "lem:representatives" (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.generator_mul_eq_zero_of_disjoint) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.generator_boundary_triple_of_same_trace) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.generator_mul_generator_mul_generator_of_same_trace) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.generator_mul_sub_generator_mul_of_same_trace) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.generator_mul_sub_generator_mul_mem_deletionRange) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.mul_mem_deletionRange) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.word_mem_deletionRange) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.generator_mul_wordBoundary_representatives)
In $`A`, the following hold.
If $`H\cap K=\varnothing` then $`e_He_K=0`.
If $`K\ne K'` and $`H\cap K=H\cap K'` is a hyperplane in $`H`, then $`e_Ke_{K'}-e_He_{K'}+e_He_K=0` and $`e_He_Ke_{K'}=0`, so $`e_He_K-e_He_{K'}` lies in the image of $`a`, and it stays there after multiplying on either side by old words.
If $`I` is a list of representatives of a nonempty dependent family in $`H`, then $`e_H\partial e_I=e_I`.

The statements of the second item in the formalization also allow $`K=K'`, in which case they hold trivially.
:::

:::node "def:lift-map" (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.DeletionCokernel) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.preRestrictionToCokernel_context) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.restrictionToCokernel) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.restrictionToCokernel_word) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.restrictionToCokernelOfRepresentatives_word) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.restrictionToCokernelOfRepresentatives_eq_restrictionToCokernel) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.cokernelGradingDecomposition) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.restrictionToCokernel_isHomogeneous)
Choosing an old representative $`K_L` for each hyperplane $`L` of the restriction, the formula above defines a homogeneous $`B`-linear map $`s:A''\to Q` of shift $`(1,1)`, and this map does not depend on the representatives.
:::

Surjectivity comes from a direct look at words, which span $`A`.
A word with no $`e_H` is old, so it vanishes in $`Q`.
A word with $`e_H` twice is zero.
Otherwise, move $`e_H` to the front with its exterior sign.
If some old factor has empty trace on $`H`, then the word is zero.
Otherwise replace each factor by the chosen representative of its trace, and if two traces coincide, the repeated generator makes the word zero.
Every remaining word is an image under $`s`.
This uses no basis theorem for $`A''`.

:::node "lem:lift-surj" (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.restrictionToCokernel_surjective)
The map $`s:A''\to Q` is surjective.
:::

# The comparison

The residue gives a map in the other direction.
Since $`rj^*=0` and $`\Phi` commutes with deletion, the map $`r\Phi_{\mathcal{A}}` vanishes on the image of $`a`, and so descends to a map $`\psi:Q\to C(U_{\mathcal{A}''})`.
The situation is the diagram
$$`\begin{array}{ccccc} A' & \xrightarrow{\ a\ } & A & \longrightarrow & Q \\ \downarrow{\scriptstyle\Phi_{\mathcal{A}'}} & & \downarrow{\scriptstyle\Phi_{\mathcal{A}}} & & \downarrow{\scriptstyle\psi} \\ C(U_{\mathcal{A}'}) & \xrightarrow{\ j^*\ } & C(U_{\mathcal{A}}) & \xrightarrow{\ r\ } & C(U_{\mathcal{A}''}) \end{array}`
whose bottom row is the short exact sequence of the first induction.

The key computation is that $`\psi s=\Phi_{\mathcal{A}''}`.
On a restricted word this is the {bp "lem:log-residue"}[residue formula], with $`[f_H]` first, and each old logarithm restricts to the logarithm of its retained hyperplane, the scalar between the equations having no effect.

Now the argument is an elementwise chase.
Let $`x\in A` with $`\Phi_{\mathcal{A}}(x)=0`.
Then $`\psi` kills the class of $`x`.
Since $`s` is surjective, that class is $`s(y)` for some $`y\in A''`, and $`\Phi_{\mathcal{A}''}(y)=\psi s(y)=0`.
By induction $`\Phi_{\mathcal{A}''}` is injective, so $`y=0`, so the class of $`x` is zero, and $`x=a(x')` for some $`x'\in A'`.
Then $`j^*\Phi_{\mathcal{A}'}(x')=\Phi_{\mathcal{A}}(a(x'))=0`.
By the first induction $`j^*` is injective, and by induction $`\Phi_{\mathcal{A}'}` is injective, so $`x'=0` and $`x=0`.

No comparison of ranks, no five lemma and no chosen inverse of $`j^*` appears, so the argument works over any coefficient ring.
It also gives more than the injectivity of $`\Phi_{\mathcal{A}}`: the map $`a` is injective, and $`s` and $`\psi` are bijective.

:::node "lem:comparison" (decl := OrlikSolomon.cokernelComparison) (decl := OrlikSolomon.bijective_of_surjective_of_injective_cokernelComparison_comp) (decl := OrlikSolomon.cokernelComparison_bijective_of_surjective_of_bijective_comp) (decl := OrlikSolomon.injective_of_comp_eq_comp) (decl := OrlikSolomon.injective_of_surjective_of_injective_cokernelComparison_comp) (decl := OrlikSolomon.linearEquivOfCokernelComparison)
Let $`a:M'\to M`, $`j:N'\to N` and $`r:N\to T` be linear maps with $`j` injective and $`rj=0`, and let $`f':M'\to N'` and $`f:M\to N` satisfy $`fa=jf'` with $`f'` bijective.
Put $`Q=M/\operatorname{im}(a)`, with $`\psi:Q\to T` induced by $`rf`.
If a surjective map $`s:L\to Q` has bijective composite $`\psi s`, then $`s` and $`\psi` are bijective and $`a` and $`f` are injective.
If $`f` is also surjective, then it is a linear equivalence.

The formalization states these conclusions separately, each with the hypotheses it needs.
:::

:::node "lem:comparison-residue" (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.deletionResidue_realization_deletionMap) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.residueComparison) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.residueComparison_cokernelMk) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.residueComparison_comp_restrictionToCokernel)
The map $`r\Phi_{\mathcal{A}}` vanishes on the image of $`a` and descends to $`\psi:Q\to C(U_{\mathcal{A}''})`, whose composite with $`s` is $`\Phi_{\mathcal{A}''}`.
:::

# The structure theorem

The theorem now follows by strong induction on the number of hyperplanes, over all finite-dimensional ambient spaces.
The empty arrangement gives $`B\simeq B`, and the chase above is the inductive step.
Since $`\Phi_{\mathcal{A}}` is surjective by generation, it is bijective, and by {bp "lem:extraction"}[homogeneous extraction] its inverse also preserves the bigrading.

```lean -show
noncomputable section
open OrlikSolomon OrlikSolomon.SmoothAlg OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra
open scoped TensorProduct DirectSum
universe u v w z
variable
  {k : Type u} [Field k]
  {R : Type v} [CommRing R]
  {B : Type w} [CommRing B] [Algebra R B]
  {ℬ : ℤ → Submodule R B} [GradedAlgebra ℬ]
  (T : OrdinaryTheory.{u, v, w, z} k ℬ)
  {V : Type u} [AddCommGroup V] [Module k V]
  [FiniteDimensional k V]
  (𝒜 : Arrangement k V)
```

:::statement "The structure theorem in Lean"
For $`k`, $`R`, $`B`, the theory `T`, the space $`V` and the arrangement `𝒜` as on the first page, the isomorphism and its bigrading are as follows.

```lean
example : OSAlgebra B 𝒜 ≃ₐ[B] T.C.obj (complement 𝒜) :=
  T.affineEquiv 𝒜

example :
    IsHomogeneousOfShift (bigrading 𝒜 ℬ)
      (T.grading (complement 𝒜)) 0 (T.affineEquiv 𝒜) :=
  T.isHomogeneousOfShift_affineEquiv 𝒜

example :
    IsHomogeneousOfShift (T.grading (complement 𝒜))
      (bigrading 𝒜 ℬ) 0 (T.affineEquiv 𝒜).symm :=
  T.isHomogeneousOfShift_affineEquiv_symm 𝒜
```

It is the unique algebra map that sends each generator to the logarithmic class of some equation of its hyperplane, for equations chosen in any way.

```lean
example (F : 𝒜 → V →ᵃ[k] k)
    (hF : ∀ H : 𝒜, IsEquation (F H) H)
    (φ : OSAlgebra B 𝒜 →ₐ[B] T.C.obj (complement 𝒜))
    (hφ : ∀ H : 𝒜, φ (generator B 𝒜 H) =
      T.logClass (complementUnit 𝒜 (F H)
        ((hF H).covers H.2))) :
    φ = T.affineEquiv 𝒜 :=
  T.toAlgHom_affineEquiv_unique F hF φ hφ
```
:::

*A word on what is conditional here.*
The second assertion of the next box is about the presentation alone: the map $`a` is injective, and $`s` identifies $`A''` with $`A/\operatorname{im}(a)`.
Yet in Lean these statements take a theory `T` with coefficient ring $`B` as an argument, because they are proved by comparison with cohomology.
So they are not established as unconditional facts of algebra for every $`B`.
They hold for every coefficient ring that carries a theory, and the library currently constructs a theory only over the zero ring.

