For every split affine arrangement, $`\Phi_{\mathcal{A}}` is a canonical bigrading-preserving $`B`-algebra equivalence from $`\operatorname{OS}_B(\mathcal{A})` to $`C(U_{\mathcal{A}})`.
For every deletion, the algebra map $`a` is injective, and $`s` identifies $`A''`, shifted by $`(1,1)`, with $`A/\operatorname{im}(a)`.
:::

# Ordinary groups and the diagonal

The structure theorem is stated over the whole of $`B`, because $`B` is the coefficient ring that the axioms provide and every map above is $`B`-linear.
The ordinary statements are read off by restricting to homogeneous components.

The piece of $`\operatorname{OS}_B(\mathcal{A})` in bidegree $`(p,w)` is $`\operatorname{OS}_R^p(\mathcal{A})\otimes_R B_{w-p}`, by {bp "lem:os-basic"}[base change], which uses no flatness.
So restricting $`\Phi_{\mathcal{A}}` to that piece gives the isomorphism for the group $`H^{p,w}(U_{\mathcal{A}})`, sending $`x\otimes b` to $`b\,\Phi(x)`.
For genuine Tate lines, this is the familiar formula $`H^p(U)\simeq\operatorname{OS}_R^p(\mathcal{A})(-p)`, and no generator of any Tate line has been chosen.

```lean
example (p : ℕ) (w : ℤ) :
    degree R 𝒜 p ⊗[R] ℬ (w - p) ≃ₗ[R]
      T.grading (complement 𝒜) ((p : ℤ), w) :=
  T.ordinaryComponentEquiv 𝒜 p w
```

Taking $`w=p` and using $`B_0\simeq R` gives the diagonal.
The realization with coefficients in $`R` is the composite of the map $`\operatorname{OS}_R(\mathcal{A})\to\operatorname{OS}_B(\mathcal{A})` with $`\Phi_{\mathcal{A}}`.
It is injective even though no flatness is available, because $`R\to B` is split over $`R` by the projection to $`B_0`, and the equivalence on each component records this.

```lean
example :
    OSAlgebra R 𝒜 ≃ₐ[R]
      ⨁ q : ℕ, diagonalGrading
        (T.grading (complement 𝒜)) q :=
  T.diagonalEquiv 𝒜
```

The statements with coefficients in $`\mathbb{Z}`, in $`\mathbb{Z}/2` and in the zero ring are instantiated in `Examples/Ordinary`, in part to test that these hypotheses are not hiding an assumption on $`2`.
The explicit basis of {bp "thm:cohom-basis"}[the first induction] transports across $`\Phi_{\mathcal{A}}` to a monomial basis of the Orlik–Solomon algebra, which is itself a result about the presentation proved through cohomology.

